# The proposal

[Use OTel Collector]: For this template's telemetry collection layer, run the upstream OpenTelemetry Collector (log collection plus an OTLP ingest point for traces and app metrics) instead of Grafana Alloy. #proposal


# Reasons for

[Use OTel Collector]
  + <Collection is where lock-in lives>: Backends are swappable — remote write and OTLP are open protocols — but the collection config is the artifact that accretes years of pipelines, transforms, and routing; writing it in the vendor-neutral standard means the layer with the highest switching cost is the one owned by a foundation, not a vendor. #pro
    - <Alloy is OTel inside>: Alloy embeds upstream Collector components and speaks OTLP natively — the lock-in is one config syntax deep, not protocol deep, so the escape hatch is a config rewrite rather than a re-instrumentation. #con
      - <The config is the asset>: A config rewrite across a fleet of clusters is exactly the migration that never gets scheduled — River-to-YAML translation of every pipeline, transform, and edge case is the real switching cost, and it grows with every cluster this template stamps. #pro
  + <Ecosystem gravity is one-directional>: SDKs, auto-instrumentation, semantic conventions, vendor docs, hiring pools, and AI-assistant training all converge on OTel-first examples; every year the gap widens, and a collector that IS the reference implementation surfs that current instead of translating it. #pro
  + <One mental model app-to-backend>: Application teams already configure receivers, processors, and exporters in their SDKs; running the same conceptual pipeline in the cluster means one vocabulary end-to-end, and a trace that misbehaves is debugged with one set of docs. #pro
  + <Neutral pairing for a neutral stack>: Alloy's differentiators — Faro RUM receiver, Loki-native pipeline stages, fleet management — pay off inside the Grafana ecosystem; paired with VictoriaMetrics-family backends those advantages mostly idle, and a Grafana-flavored collector in a non-Grafana stack is an oddity someone eventually asks to justify. #pro
  + <Kubernetes presets are maintained upstream>: The Collector's Helm chart ships curated presets (log collection, Kubernetes attribute enrichment) that encode the fiddly parts — hostPath mounts, checkpointing, downward-API wiring — and are maintained by the community that maintains the components themselves. #pro

# Reasons against

[Use OTel Collector]
  - <Alloy is the friendlier operator>: One curated binary, a typed config language whose pipelines are explicit data flows, and a built-in UI for live pipeline debugging; the Collector answers with sprawling YAML, and misconfigurations that surface at runtime rather than at load. #con
    - <Curation cuts both ways>: The typed config and curated set are also a private language — River expertise transfers nowhere else, while Collector YAML fluency is the industry's shared skill and every example on the internet is written in it. #pro
  - <The distribution matrix is a footgun>: Core, contrib, and k8s images ship different component sets, and the chart demands an explicit image choice — pick wrong and your exporter simply is not in the binary; Alloy ships one artifact with everything included. #con
    - <A template chooses once>: The matrix is a decision this repo makes one time, records in a comment, and every stamped cluster inherits — a real sharp edge, but one that boilerplate exists to pre-blunt. #pro
  - <Log collection lineage favors Alloy>: Alloy inherits the promtail bloodline — years of CRI parsing, Kubernetes discovery, and multiline-handling hardening at fleet scale; the Collector's filelog receiver is capable but has fewer miles on exactly the path this template uses most. #con
  - <RUM lands Alloy-first>: Grafana's Faro web SDK expects Alloy's faro receiver; Collector-side support exists in contrib but arrived later and is less proven, so a future real-user-monitoring pipeline is smoother on the incumbent. #con
    - <RUM can stay OTLP too>: Browser telemetry has an OTel JS path, and a single small Alloy deployment can always be added later solely as a Faro receiver without making Alloy the cluster's collection layer — the niche need does not have to pick the general tool. #pro
  - <The working pipeline is Alloy>: This template ships a functioning Alloy config today — CRI parsing, JSON level extraction, structured-metadata promotion — and its replacement must re-earn that behavior in a different idiom; some niceties (per-field structured-metadata promotion) need explicit transform processors the preset does not write for you. #con
    - <Boilerplate pays migration cost once>: The rewrite happens once, in this repo, reviewed — not per team or per cluster; stamped environments get whichever layer wins with zero switching cost of their own. #pro
  - <Contrib quality is uneven>: The contrib distribution's hundreds of components span alpha-to-stable maturity with varying ownership; Alloy's curated set is smaller but uniformly supported by one accountable vendor. #con

# Cross-cutting context

[Backends are VictoriaMetrics-family]: This collector ships as part of the VictoriaMetrics/VictoriaLogs/Tempo alternative stack — no Grafana backend is in the data path, so Alloy's ecosystem synergies have nothing to attach to and neutrality costs nothing. #context
  +> <Neutral pairing for a neutral stack>
  -> <RUM lands Alloy-first>

[Backends are Grafana LGTM]: If the cluster instead runs the Loki/Tempo/Mimir family end-to-end, Alloy's Loki-native stages, Faro receiver, and single-vendor coherence are live advantages and this proposal loses most of its force. #context
  -> [Use OTel Collector]
  +> <Alloy is the friendlier operator>


# A common alternative

[Run Alloy as an OTel pipeline]: Keep Alloy as the binary but configure it purely through its embedded otelcol components — OTLP in, OTLP out — buying Alloy's operational ergonomics while keeping pipelines conceptually portable. #alternative
  -> [Use OTel Collector]
  - <Portable concepts, captive config>: The pipeline shape is portable but the artifact still is not — it is River, versioned on Grafana's cadence, translated by hand on exit — so the alternative softens the lock-in argument without answering it. #con
  + <Best of both during transition>: For an estate already invested in Alloy, expressing new pipelines otelcol-style inside the incumbent is a zero-disruption way to converge on OTel semantics before any binary swap. #pro
